  "image_name": "0e8ba326b73f082a273b6aff953eb3c6.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how RNA helicases and translation factors coordinate mRNA translation with RNA quality control and decay. At the 5′ end, the cap and poly(A) tail are linked by initiation factors such as eIF4E, eIF4G, eIF4A, PABP, and associated proteins including DDX3, eIF4B/eIF4H, and PDCD4. These factors promote recruitment of the 43S preinitiation complex and formation of the 48S complex during translation initiation.\n\nAlong the mRNA, secondary structures and especially G-quadruplexes are shown as obstacles that require helicases like DHX29, DHX36, and DHX9 to resolve so scanning can proceed. The lower part of the diagram shifts to translation elongation and RNA decay, showing ribosome-associated surveillance pathways. A premature stop codon recruits UPF1 and several effectors, indicating nonsense-mediated decay. Other decay routes involve deadenylation and decapping, connected to factors such as SKIV2L, DDX6, CCR4–NOT, and IGHMBP2.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that mRNA translation is not just ribosome loading and movement: it is tightly coupled to helicase activity, unfolding of structured RNA, and multiple decay pathways that remove defective or poorly translated transcripts.",
